 @dra6o0n  We're not even considering the idle power draw here, which is very much a thing that happens on both manufacturers GPUs. That's something I'm not going to deny, I definitely don't appreciate you putting it as if I ever claimed that.

What is however an issue is designing a cooler that by design lets memory modules overheat, something that was an issue with Nvidia reference designs for the 30XX series as well.

The melting power cables are very much user error it seems, I.E. something that can be avoided by connecting your GPU properly. A not well performing cooling solution however has no real fix outside of replacing it, so long as AMD doesn't offer that, it's as much of a don't buy it advice as it is for reference 30XX GPUs from Nvidia. The problem here is that the better designed AMD GPUs (AIB models) for this generation happen to be seriously marked up to the point that it's no longer even close to MSRP. I.E. as lacklustre in value as Nvidia 40XX GPUs are right now.

 @vigilant_1934  Man, you are the really hard AMD fan! For your information and anyone else who reads this text, what the Gamer Nexus showed with certainty was that the 12VHPWR connector, when inserted very poorly, would heat up and melt. And, guess what! Me and a few other people, with a few years of experience in life, were already saying that the images of burnt 12VHPWR plugs that were seen on Reedit had all the appearance of electrical plugs damaged by the heat resulting from being incorrectly inserted, something that it happens in all places where there is electrical equipment (I've seen this happen on the switch on a dishwasher and on one of the electrical plugs on the alternator of one of my motorcycles). However, the young people didn't pay any attention to what we said, a mistake we also made when we were younger. But the biggest problem was that almost all YouTubers, especially those who earn money from views on their channels, made it a great opportunity to fuel a controversy that would bring them more views on their YouTube channels and, consequentely, more money. And, as expected, AMD fanatics enjoy the ride, because envy is something that bites very deep.

As for the issue of existing debris in the plug, it was pointed out as a possible manufacturing defect that would aggravate the heating problem if the plug was inserted incorrectly or worn out from being turned off and on too many times. Nowhere in this video is connector debris claimed to be the sole cause of 12VHPWR connectors melting.

And more! The 12VHPWR connector has already been used on the RTX 3090Ti without major problems and since Gamer Nexus released the video showing how the connector melted, we no longer see people complaining about the same problem. I wonder why? Was it because the owners of the RTX 4090 went to find out if the 12VHPWR plugs on their cards were actually well inserted?

